"""Point-by-point response to the four comments in the current request."""
R=Document();style_doc(R)
section=R.sections[0]
section.top_margin=Inches(.75);section.bottom_margin=Inches(.75)
section.left_margin=Inches(.8);section.right_margin=Inches(.8)
R.add_paragraph('Response to Reviewer on Scenario Robust FSO Beam Design',style='Title')
R.add_paragraph(
    'We have clarified the specific technical contribution, added a comparison with recent FSO beam optimization, separated literature-supported model choices from uncalibrated stress parameters, expanded the blockage evaluation, and implemented an independent numerical validation approach. All manuscript additions and substantive changes are highlighted in light green. The response below identifies the evidence and its limits. The accompanying archive contains the numerical code, exact seeds, raw results, figures, and source-verification notes.')

items=[
('1 Novelty and comparison with recent FSO beam optimization',
 'Reviewer comment: The novelty is not clearly established; compare recent FSO beam optimization methods and highlight the technical contribution.',
 f'Response: The Introduction, abstract, contribution paragraph, and conclusion now define the contribution as a receiver-availability-conditioned minimax lower-tail formulation for static beam-center allocation, with optional divergence refinement. Each prescribed aperture-loss state first receives a conditional 5% lower-tail power mean; the minimum of these statewise scores drives the design. Exact Gaussian coupling, differential evolution, CVaR, and joint beam control are explicitly not claimed as individually new.\n\n'
 f'Table {table_number_map[11]} compares Cha et al. [11] (2024), Park et al. [12] (2025), Ling et al. [13] (2026), and Lim et al. [25] (2026), including controls, objectives, and the type of validation each reports. The text acknowledges prior robust pointing and experimentally demonstrated joint multi-beam control, rather than presenting all prior work as mean-only or unvalidated. Earlier blockage-aware FSO analysis [39] is also acknowledged.\n\n'
 f'Section 5.12 and Table {table_number_map[10]} provide objective-level numerical comparisons with average-SNR and average-outage criteria motivated by [11,13], alongside mean-power design, under the same seven-state channel, footprint bounds, launch power, receiver model, SAA budget, and optimizer. These are adaptations of criteria rather than reproductions of those papers’ different hardware/architectures. In S6, the joint-tail mean is 0.08741 versus 0.04565 for the re-optimized mean-power design, a 91.49% increase with 32.72% lower mean collection. The paper also reports that this power-tail objective does not minimize every BER/outage measure at every launch power. This establishes the specific contribution and its trade-off without asserting universal superiority.'),
('2 Scintillation and blockage parameter justification',
 'Reviewer comment: The assumed parameters lack experimental justification; support them with literature or measurements and discuss their impact.',
 f'Response: Section 2.3 and Table {table_number_map[12]} now state which assumptions have literature support and which remain deliberately selected stress values. Newly added [37] reports a lognormal fit to normalized irradiance on a 220 m measured FSO link. The recent four-aperture, 865 m experimental study [38] supplies additional context for irradiance statistics and aperture-diversity mitigation. These external measurements are not represented as measurements of our terminal or as fitted values for rc, bblock, bmicro, or Pmicro. The weak-Rytov interpretation of σln = 0.38, scintillation index 0.15535, and equivalent Cn² ≈ 7.25 × 10⁻¹⁵ m⁻²ᐟ³ remains explicit in Section 2.4, with finite-beam/aperture-averaging qualifications.\n\n'
 'The 55 mm length is a phenomenological latent log-irradiance correlation scale, not a measured Fried/coherence length. The Uniform multipliers denote retained optical power, and the 1% micro-event probability is per aperture per realization conditional on each receiver state. Reference [39] motivates blockage-aware analysis but does not establish the chosen distributions or endpoints. We do not invent an experimental calibration for those values. A concrete terminal-calibration procedure is described.\n\n'
 f'Section 5.14, Table {table_number_map[13]}, and Fig. 12 add six independent 20,000-sample/state trials for each of eleven one-at-a-time settings: σln = 0.15, 0.38, 0.65; rc = 20, 55, 120 mm; pair retained-power upper endpoint 0, 0.12, 0.30; micro-event probability 0%, 1%, 5%; and micro-retention upper endpoint 0.1, 0.4, 0.8. Retained designs are not retuned. The S6 joint-tail mean ranges from 0.06934 to 0.09707. For example, increasing rc from 20 to 120 mm lowers it from 0.09078 to 0.07874, and increasing micro-event probability from zero to 5% lowers it from 0.08907 to 0.08208. The mean-power comparison and 95% intervals are reported for every setting. We explicitly limit this evidence to the tested parameter ranges; the sweep is not measured uncertainty or joint-uncertainty validation.'),
('3 Additional blockage patterns and worst case performance',
 'Reviewer comment: Four receiver states are insufficient; evaluate additional patterns and compare worst-case performance.',
 f'Response: Sections 2.3, 5.7, and 5.10 distinguish S3 (no pair blockage plus the original three blocked pairs) from S6 (no pair blockage plus all six adjacent outer pairs). Anisotropic pointing and the shifted footprint prevent treating omitted rotations as equivalent. Pair (4,5), omitted from S3, lies opposite pair (1,2) on the major pointing axis and exposes a weakness in the historical design.\n\n'
 f'Section 5.7 and Table {table_number_map[6]} evaluate the retained S3 vectors on every adjacent outer pair, all 21 distinct two-aperture pairs including central-aperture losses, and a fresh randomized adjacent-sector mixture. Across ten independent expanded-pattern trials, the worst-of-21 L5 is 0.06767 (95% CI 0.06718–0.06817) for the historical joint-tail footprint versus 0.04614 (0.04550–0.04678) for its mean-power baseline. The relative worst-case advantage is 46.68%, rather than transferring the original 119.07% headline unchanged. The footprint does not dominate on every individual pair.\n\n'
 f'Section 5.10 and Table {table_number_map[8]} then re-optimize on S6, rather than evaluating only the old footprint. Five independent SAA/optimizer seed pairs are reported, including final controls, training scores, and independent-trial intervals. The predeclared retained S6 joint design gives 0.08741 (0.08687–0.08795), compared with 0.06823 (0.06735–0.06912) for the historical S3 joint vector evaluated on the same S6 trial pools. The separate randomized mixture is clearly distinguished from the minimum of conditional statewise tails. Larger sectors, arbitrary shadow shapes, and all-pair optimality of the new S6 vector are not claimed.'),
('4 Independent simulation validation and experimental scope',
 'Reviewer comment: Validate the simulation results using an independent approach or experimental data, if available.',
 f'Response: Section 5.15, Eq. (13), Table {table_number_map[14]}, and Fig. 13 add a separate end-to-end numerical evaluator. green_independent_solver.py imports none of the original simulator’s coupling, random generator, or tail functions. It integrates the two-dimensional Gaussian intensity over each circular aperture using Gauss–Legendre/periodic angular quadrature, generates Gaussian channels using symmetric eigendecomposition, draws the complete stochastic channel with independently scrambled 30-dimensional Sobol sequences, and obtains the tail mean by full sorting. The original route instead uses noncentral-chi-square coupling, Cholesky-based pseudorandom generation, and partition-based tails. Both target the same stated physical assumptions and use fixed retained controls.\n\n'
 'The study checks quadrature order and off-grid interpolation explicitly. Over 276 displacement points per footprint, refined direct integration differs from analytic coupling by at most 1.67 × 10⁻¹⁶; the 0.2 mm interpolation grid contributes at most 7.27 × 10⁻⁸ in the checked coupling fraction. Paired 8,192-sample tests in each state show maximum power-factor discrepancy 2.98 × 10⁻⁷ and statewise tail discrepancy 4.58 × 10⁻¹¹. Geometry and covariance entries agree exactly.\n\n'
 f'Fresh independent calculations compare both approaches at 4,096, 16,384, and 65,536 samples in every S6 state, with eight independent trials or scrambles per budget. At the largest budget, the joint S6 design gives 0.08782 for the independent approach versus 0.08791 for analytic coupling/Monte Carlo ({independent_relative_percent["S6_joint_alpha05"]:.3f}%). Across four fixed footprints, the largest absolute relative difference is {max(relative):.3f}%, each independent mean falls inside the corresponding analytic-Monte-Carlo 95% interval, and the footprint ordering is preserved at all three budgets. All raw trial results, quadrature checks, seeds, and code are provided.\n\n'
 'This directly addresses independent computational verification. It is not experimental validation of the Gaussian/lognormal/blockage laws, and the manuscript states that distinction in its abstract, limitations, conclusion, and this section. No matching measured terminal dataset or hardware experiment is introduced. The published experiments cited for context are not relabelled as validation of our design. Wave-optics and field calibration remain necessary before deployment or availability claims.'),
]
for title,comment,response in items:
    R.add_paragraph(title,style='Heading 1')
    p=R.add_paragraph(comment)
    for r in p.runs:r.italic=True
    for text in response.split('\n\n'):
        p=R.add_paragraph(text)
        for r in p.runs:font_run(r,11,highlight=True)
R.add_paragraph('Presentation and reproducibility checks',style='Heading 1')
p=R.add_paragraph(
    'The repository URL is corrected consistently, angle bounds explicitly use μrad, all aperture indices follow 0–6, and captions/table numbering follow manuscript order. Tables have repeated headers and non-splitting rows. The 119.07% repeated-trial result is distinguished from the original 119.35% single-test result. Native editable equations are retained, and all manuscript changes use light green shading. The archive includes the earlier S3/S6 studies as well as the new sensitivity and independent-validation code, with exact settings and raw numerical evidence.')
for r in p.runs:font_run(r,11,highlight=True)
convert_to_green(R)
for p in R.paragraphs:
    for r in p.runs:
        font_run(r,16 if p.style.name=='Title' else (12 if p.style.name.startswith('Heading') else 11),
            bold=True if p.style.name=='Title' or p.style.name.startswith('Heading') else None)
R.core_properties.title='Response to Reviewer on Scenario Robust FSO Beam Design'
R.core_properties.comments='Response to four reviewer comments; manuscript changes are light green.'
R.save(OUT/'FSO_Response_to_Reviewer_Light_Green.docx')
